[Event "Caro-Kann Chapter 9: Intro & Variation A (6. Bxh6)"]
[Site "Chess Trainer"]
[Date "2026.02.24"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Book"]
[Black "Hero"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5!? {In the past the Advance Variation was considered to be a timid variation that allowed Black to obtain a ‘good’ version of the French Advance, a good version because the light-squared bishop was able to develop outside the pawn chain. This assessment has dramatically changed in recent years and now the Advance is one of the most popular lines against the Caro-Kann due to White’s natural space advantage. New ideas keep sprouting at a fast and furious rate and in the 3...Bf5 line Black must be prepared to meet many lines, from the hyper-aggressive 4 Nc3 and 4 h4 to the more sophisticated Short system with 4 Nf3. All these lines are highly theoretical and in order to escape the theoretical battles I will again focus on my original recommendation 3...c5!?. Our approach is designed to challenge White immediately and take him out of the mentality of building up a slow advantage with a stable centre.}
4. dxc5 {Accepting the offered pawn will swing the game into complications where Black has the superior pawn structure and White’s weakened e5-pawn can become a target. Taking the pawn is pretty complicated variation, in particular when Black is happy to play a pawn down for an attack. One thing I want to stress in this particular variation is that we have a recurring theme of undermining White's pawn structure, rather than attacking the weakened pawns with pieces.}
4... e6 {The advantage of playing ...c6-c5 as early as move three is that White must take critical decisions very early on, which is not something an unprepared white player will be ready for.}
    ( 4... Nc6!? {I would also recommend the reader investigate the complex 4...Nc6!?. The resulting play is again very double-edged, but Black can develop his bishop to g4.} )
5. Be3 {The major line and the most logical choice. White protects the c5-pawn and develops a piece at the same time.}
    ( 5. b4?! {Note that 5 b4 doesn't hold on to the pawn} 5... Nc6 6. c3 Nxe5! )
5... Nh6 {Why does Black develop a knight to the edge of the board? Well, the knight is heading to f5 from where it will exert pressure on the bishop on e3. White now has only a limited choice of good moves: 6 Bxh6, 6 c3, or 6 Nf3.}
6. Bxh6 {Our first critical try.}
    ( 6. f4? {White is simply not careful enough and 6...Nd7! hits the c5-pawn.} 6... Nd7!
        ( 7. b4 a5! 8. c3 axb4 9. cxb4 b6! 10. Bb5 {7 b4 looks natural, but White is rudely awakened after 7...a5! 8 c3 axb4 9 cxb4 b6! 10 Bb5 bxc5 and taking the pawn is impossible because of the loose bishop on b5.} 10... bxc5 11. Bc6 Rb8 12. bxc5 Nxc5 13. Bxc5 Qa5+! {Black recovers the piece with an excellent position to boot.} )
        ( 7. Bb5 Qa5+ 8. Nc3 a6! 9. Bxd7+ Bxd7 10. Nge2 ( 10. a3 Qc7 11. b4 b6! ) 10... Nf5 11. Bf2 Bxc5 {and Black already had the upper hand in M.Nimtz-I.Khenkin, Bad Wiessee 2000.} )
    )
    ( 6. Bd3 {This was played against me very recently, but it is not well thought out.} 6... Nc6 7. c4? {Black's point is that 7 Nf3 Ng4 regains the material and even more.} ( 7. Bxh6 gxh6 {had to be played} ) 7... dxc4 8. Be4 Qxd1+ 9. Kxd1 Nxe5 10. Na3 f5 11. Bd4 fxe4 12. Bxe5 Ng4 13. Nxc4 Nxf2+ 14. Ke2 Nxh1 15. b4 b6 {and now in E.Kvisvik-J.Houska, Fagernes 2014, 15...b6 would have won the game in an easy manner.} )
6... gxh6 {Technically speaking this is the principled line, but it is hardly ever played. In compensation for the doubled h-pawns Black has the two bishops and will almost certainly recover the lost pawn. In the battle for activity the doubled h-pawns are not a weakness; in fact they help Black as he now has the half-open g-file to use.}
7. Nd2 {Alternatively:}
    ( 7. b4?! a5!? {Black should commence active operations to open lines and diagonals without delay. White holds on to the extra pawn for dear life, but with no regard for the consequences.} 8. c3 axb4 9. cxb4 b6! {Black begins to break up the white pawns and his two bishops can be happy:}
        ( 10. Nf3 bxc5 11. bxc5 Bxc5 {Black intends to apply heavy pressure on the white king as quickly as possible.} 12. Bd3 Qa5+ 13. Nbd2 Ba6! {Now in order to castle White will have to allow some exchanges to take place, which is something that will benefit Black as the a-pawn is more of a liability than a strength.} 14. Qe2 Qc3! 15. Bb5+ Nd7 16. O-O Bxf2+!? 17. Qxf2 Bxb5 18. Rfc1 Qa5 19. Qg3 Qb6+ 20. Kh1 Bd3! 21. Qg7 Ke7 22. Rc3 {and Black has no problems.} )
        ( 10. Bb5+ Bd7 11. Bxd7+ Qxd7! {hitting the two pawns, c5 and e5} 12. Nf3 bxc5 13. bxc5 Qc7 14. O-O Bg7! {a good location for the bishop, eyeing the e5-pawn and at the same time giving some shelter to the black king for when it goes short.} 15. Nbd2 Nxe5 {Black has a great position and it is apparent that the doubled pawns hardly matter at all.} )
    )
    ( 7. Nf3 Nc6 {is a safe line and now if White tries to place Black under pressure with 8 c4...} 8. c4 ( 8. c3 Bxc5! 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Nc2 ( 10. Nc3 Black should seize space with 10...d4 11. Ne4 Bb4+ 12. Nfd2 O-O 13. f4 Bf5 14. Nf6+ Kh8 15. a3 Be7 16. Nde4 Bxf6 17. Nxf6 d3 {preventing White's bishop from developing} ) 10... Qa5+ 11. Nbd2?! Nb4 12. Qb3 Bf5! {Black develops his bishop to the most active square and suddenly White must figure out an adequate way of dealing with the threat of 13...Nc2+.} 13. Rc1 Qb6! {now the f2-pawn is a target} 14. Be2 Bxf2+ 15. Kf1 Rd8 {Black has the better chances.} ) )
7... Nc6!? {This is not a normal idea as usually Black rushes to win the c5-pawn with an immediate ...Bxc5. However, we are after bigger fish on e5. Moreover, taking with 7...Bxc5 did not prove so successful for Black in K.Hansen-J.Andersen, correspondence 2003.}
8. Nf3 Bd7 {Preventing any Bb5 ideas that may hamper Black's ideas of piling pressure on the e5-pawn.}
9. Bd3 Qc7 10. Qe2 Bg7 {Now the e-pawn can no longer be defended.}
11. c3 Nxe5 12. Nxe5 Bxe5 13. O-O-O O-O-O 14. Nb3 Ba4 {Black is fine here.} *

[Event "Caro-Kann Chapter 9: Variation B1 (9. Be2)"]
[Site "Chess Trainer"]
[Date "2026.02.24"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Book"]
[Black "Hero"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. dxc5 e6 5. Be3 Nh6 6. c3 {Preparation for White's next.}
6... Nf5 7. Bd4 {Generally speaking Black has a little bit of a formula going on here: play ...Nfxd4 followed by ...b7-b6, putting pressure on the b2- and d4-pawns. When the white bishop is on d3 this formula is especially effective, but I have to stress that we do not use this formula in the main lines, i.e. when White has prepared the defence of the c5-pawn with a future b2-b4.}
7... Nd7! {Instead, after 7 Qd2 Nd7 8 Bb5 a6 9 Ba4 Qc7 White cannot hold on to the pawn for too long. Let us see what happens if Black employ the formula too early:}
    ( 7... Nxd4?! 8. cxd4 b6 9. Bb5+! Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Qxd7 11. cxb6 axb6 12. Ne2 Bb4+ 13. Nbc3 Qc6 14. O-O O-O 15. Qd3 {Black has nothing for the pawn.} )
    ( 7... Nc6 {The Formula: ...Nfxd4 then ...b6} 8. Nf3 ( 8. Qd2 Nd7 9. Bb5 a6 10. Ba4 Qc7 ) ( 8. g4?! Nxd4! 9. cxd4 b6! 10. cxb6 Qxb6 11. Qd2 Nc6 12. Nf3 h5 13. gxh5 Rb8 14. Nc3 ( 14. b3 Bb4 15. Nc3 Rc8 ) 14... Qxb2 15. Rb1 Qxd2+ 16. Kxd2 Rxb1 17. Nxb1 Rxh5 {Black will be playing on for a long time, grinding out a win.} ) ( 8. Bd3 Nxd4 9. cxd4 b6! 10. cxb6 Qxb6 11. Ne2 ( 11. Nf3 Nc6 ) 11... Nc6 12. Nbc3 Nxd4 13. O-O Nc6 14. Bb5 Nxe5 15. Nxd5 Qxb7 16. Bxd7+ Nxd7 17. Ndc3 Be7 18. Qd2 {This is our dream position. Black will regain the pawn with the long-term advantage of the two bishops.} ) )
8. Nf3 Nc6! {The main line. I must strongly stress that against 9 Qd2 and 9 a3 we are not following the formula.}
    ( 8... Nxd4 9. cxd4 b6! 10. cxb6 Qxb6 11. Ne2 Nc6 12. Nbc3 Nxd4 13. O-O Nc6 14. Bb5 Nxe5 15. Nxd5 Qxb7 16. Bxd7+ Nxd7 17. Ndc3 Be7 18. Qd2 {Black was better in Y.Zhou-J.Speelman, British League 2014.} )
9. Be2 {B1. Once again we can follow our favourite formula.}
9... Nfxd4 10. cxd4 b6 11. cxb6 Qxb6 12. b3 {After 12 Nc3 Qxb2 13 Nb5 Bb4+ 14 Nd2 a5 15 O-O Nxe5 Black is fine and 12 Qd2 doesn't solve White's problems: 12...Rb8! 13 b3 Bb4 14 Nc3 Qa5 15 Rc1 and now after 15...Ne7 White must return the material.}
12... Bb4+ 13. Kf1 O-O 14. g3 f6! {Thematically opening lines when the white king’s safety is questionable.}
15. exf6 Rxf6 16. Kg2 Raf8 17. Rf1 Bd3 {This was A.Abreu-G.Siegel, Havana 1998, and now Black missed 17...Rxf3!.}
    ( 17... Rxf3! 18. Bxf3 Nxd4 19. Bg4 ( 19. g4 b5 20. Nd2! ( 20. Re1? Rxf2+ 21. Kxf2 Qc2+ 22. Kg2 Nxe1+ {wins on the spot} ) 20... Bd3 {White is completely tied down} ) 19... b5 20. Nd2 Be1? 21. Rxf2 Qc2+ 22. Kg2 Qxe1+ )
*

[Event "Caro-Kann Chapter 9: Variation B2 (9. Qd2)"]
[Site "Chess Trainer"]
[Date "2026.02.24"]
