[White "Book"]
[Black "Hero"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. dxc5 e6 5. Be3 Nh6 6. c3 Nf5 7. Bd4 Nd7! 8. Nf3 Nc6!
9. Qd2 {A very important variation particularly in light of the fact that we cannot follow the formula. This is primarily because White is preparing the defensive pawn push b2-b4. Black should instead play in another active way.}
9... f6! {Unfortunately White is well prepared for the formula, as shown by 9...Nfxd4 10 cxd4 a5 (10...b6 11 b4! is the whole point of 9 Qd2).}
10. exf6?! {White is too cautious and gives up the pawn centre. Black can now look forward to pushing the centre pawns.}
    ( 10. b4 {B22. White chooses to overprotect the extra c-pawn, leaving the central tension intact.} 10... Nfxd4! {Rightly eliminating the tension in the centre before swapping on d4.} 11. cxd4 fxe5 12. dxe5 Qc7 {Preparing to attack the c5-pawn after the exchanges.} 13. Qb2 ( 13. Nc3 Nxe5 14. Nd4 Be7 15. Nb5 Qb8 16. f4 a6! 17. Nd6+ Bxd6 18. cxd6 Nc6 {Black stands well.} ) ( 13. Qc3 a5 14. b5 d4 15. b6 Qxb6 16. cxb6 dxc3 17. Nxc3 Bb4 18. Rc1 Rc8 {Black has a lot of pressure for the pawn.} ) 13... a6! {An excellent defensive move, preventing the pawn push b4-b5.} 14. Be2 {In light of what now happens White may be tempted to play 14 h4 g6 15 h5 g5! 16 Nxg5, but here Black has the tactic 16...Nxb4!.} 14... g6! {Attacking the vulnerable e5-pawn from another angle.} 15. Nbd2 Bg7 16. Nb3 O-O 17. O-O Nxe5 {Black has an excellent position here with the possibility of pushing hard in the centre.} 18. Rac1 Nc6 19. a4?! f4 20. b5 b4 21. Qe3 axb5 22. axb5 Nd4 23. Nxd4 Bxd4 24. Qg5 Qxc5 25. Nf3 b6 26. Ne5 Bxb5 {Black is winning.} )
    ( 10. Bd3 {This is simply no good and forces us to play the good 10...Nfxd4.} 10... Nfxd4 11. cxd4 fxe5 12. Nxe5 Nxe5 13. dxe5 Bxc5 14. O-O Qh4 15. Qe2 O-O {Black was clearly better in K.Bryzgalin-P.Prizant, Russia 2000.} )
10... gxf6 11. Be3 {Black can now look forward to pushing the centre pawns.}
    ( 11. Be2?! {This is rather tame.} 11... e5! 12. Be3 Nxe3 13. fxe3 ( 13. Qxe3 had to be played even though Black wins material with 13...Qe7! ) 13... Bxc5 14. Qxd5 Qe7 15. Nbd2 O-O 16. Ne4 Bxe3 {A big success for Black in C.Tesik-D.Berczes, Budapest 2012.} )
    ( 11. Bb5 {More purposeful.} 11... Qe7! 12. b4 {White is vulnerable to attacks on the g-file.} 12... Rg8 13. O-O ( 13. g3 e5! 14. Be3? d4! {A huge blunder.} ) 13... Bh6 14. Be3 Nxe3 15. fxe3 d4 16. cxd4 exd4 17. Qxd4 18. Kh1 Bxe3 {Black is much better.} )
11... Qe7 {Preparing to attack the c5-pawn after the exchanges.} 12. b4 a5 13. a3 Nxe3 14. fxe3 Bh6 15. Bb5 O-O 16. O-O axb4 17. cxb4 d4! {Now Black has a tactical strike.} 18. Nxd4 Nxd4 19. Qxd7 Nb3 20. Qc3 Nxa1 21. Bb5 Qc7!? {Or 21...b6 22 cxb6 Qd6 and White is struggling here.} 22. Qxa1 Bxe3+ 23. Kh1 Qe5 24. Qa2 {White was battling to fight for equality in O.Hartig-P.Dittmar, Gibraltar 2007.} *

[Event "Caro-Kann Chapter 9: Variation B3 (9. a3)"]
[Site "Chess Trainer"]
[Date "2026.02.24"]
