[Result "*"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. dxc5 e6 5. Be3 Nh6 6. c3 Nf5 7. Bd4 Nd7 8. Nf3 Nc6 9. a3 {As we have seen, when White prepares the defensive pawn move b2-b4 Black cannot play the immediate formula of ...Nfxd4 followed by ...b7-b6.}
9... a5! {Preventing b4. I believe Black should renew the threat with this move.}
    ( 9... f6!? 10. exf6 gxf6 11. Ng5! {This is possible as White threatens a check on h5. This may be a position that only computers are prepared to play.} )
10. Be2 {Important alternatives are 10 a4!? or 10 Bd3.}
    ( 10. a4?! {Should White play the positional 10 a4!?, securing the b5-square for the bishop, Black should respond carefully with 10...cxd4!.} 10... cxd4! 11. cxd4 b6! {Now that the b5-square is covered, Black can open lines.} 12. cxb6 Qxb6 13. Bb5 Bd7 {Black has recovered the pawn.} ( 13. Na7? 14. Bxd7+ Kxd7 15. O-O Qxb2 16. Nbd2 Qb6 {Black was in serious trouble in T.Gelashvili-T.Woodward, Guernsey 2002.} ) 14. O-O O-O 15. Nc3 {a1) Holding on to the pawn with 12 cxb6?! allows 12...Qxb6 13 Qd2 Rb8! and the pawn falls anyhow.} )
    ( 10. Bd3 {Another option to which we should reply with the formulaic 10...Nfxd4.} 10... Nfxd4 11. cxd4 b6 12. cxb6 Qxb6 13. Nc3 Nxd4 14. O-O Be7 {Black has recovered the pawn.} ( 14. O-O bxc5 15. dxc5 Bxc5 16. Nc3 Nd4 17. Nxd4 Bxd4 18. Qe2 Qc7 19. Rfe1 Rb8 20. a4 O-O {Again Black stands well.} ) ( 14. Qc2 bxc5 15. dxc5 Qe7 16. Nc3 Qxc5 17. O-O Rb8 18. Rac1 Be7 19. Qe2 Qb6 20. Na4 d4 21. Nxd4 Nxd4 {With zero problems.} ) )
10... Be7 11. O-O O-O 12. Qd3 {If 12 Bd3 now is the time to push the a4-pawn with 12...a4.}
    ( 12. Bd3 a4! 13. Bxf5 exf5 14. Qd3 f4! {A multi-dimensional move, releasing the d7-bishop and also tying the bishop to d4; from now on it becomes tricky for White to think up a plan.} 15. Nbd2 ( 15. Qd2 a5 16. Nxf4 Nb3 17. Ra2 Bb5 18. Rd1 Be2 19. Re1 Bc4 20. Be3 {The white rook is trapped on a2.} ) 15... Qc8 16. Rfe1 Bf5 17. Qe2 Re8 18. c4 Qd7 19. Rad1 Rad8 20. cxd5 Qxd5 21. Nc4 Bg4 {White is struggling.} )
12... a4! {Black aims to obtain long-term compensation for when we play ...b7-b6.}
13. Nbd2 b6! {White also has to be careful that Black can't able to slip into old ways. Indeed, after 12 Nbd2 the defence of the d4-pawn is interrupted.}
    ( 13. b3 {Designed to prevent ...a5-a4, but allows Black to play 12...f6!.} 13... f6! 14. Bd3 fxe5 15. Nxe5 Nfxd4 16. cxd4 Nxe5 17. dxe5 g6 18. Qc2 Kg7 19. Nd2 Rc8 20. b4 b6 21. Ba6 Rc7 {The c-pawn will fall.} )
14. cxb6 Ba6 15. c4 {14 b4 is another possibility and after 14...axb3 15 Nxb3 Na5! 16 Nxa5 Rxa5 17 cxb6 Nxd4 18 cxd4 Qxb6 19 Rfb1 Qa7 it will only be a matter of time before the a-pawn is rounded up.}
15... dxc4 16. Qe4 Qd5 17. Rac1 {After 17 b4 axb3 18 Rxb3 Qd8 19 Rc3 Qf8 the pressure on a3 is too much for White to bear.}
17... c3! 18. Rxc3 Bxe2 19. Qxe2 Ncxd4 20. Nxd4 Nxd4 21. Nxd4 Qxd4 {Black is doing very well as the bishop rules over the white knight on a3.} *

[Event "Caro-Kann Chapter 9: Variation C (6. Nf3)"]
[Site "Chess Trainer"]
[Date "2026.02.24"]
[Round "5"]
[White "Book"]
[Black "Hero"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. dxc5 e6 5. Be3 Nh6 6. Nf3 {A tricky move. White returns the c-pawn and lets Black get in ...Nh6-f5 for free, but says to Black, what are you doing with your bishop on c8? This approach does, however, look suspicious to me.}
6... Nf5 {A less popular move than 6...Nd7.}
7. Bg5! {Of course, the best. Let's just have a quick look at the alternatives 7 Bd4?!, 7 Bb5+, or 7 Qd2.}
    ( 7. Bd4?! {Just looks dubious to me.} 7... Nd7 ( 7... Nxd4 {Is, of course, fully playable still.} ) 8. c3 Nc6 9. Bd3?! Nfxd4 10. cxd4 b6 11. O-O bxc5 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. Nc3 Qe7 14. a3 a5 15. Rc1 O-O 16. Rb1 Bb8 17. Qd3 g6 18. Qe2 Rd8 19. Bd3 Bf8 20. h4 Bg7 21. Rfe1 h5 {With an instructive and good game for Black in R.Polzin-F.Vallejo Pons, Hamburg 2010.} )
    ( 7. Bb5+ {Does not make so much sense since White exchanges Black's worst piece on the board.} 7... Bd7 8. Bxd7+ Nxd7 9. Bg5 Qc7 {Black will win back one of his pawns with equality.} )
    ( 7. Qd2 Nxe3 8. Qxe3 Qa5+ 9. c3 Bxc5 10. Qd2 Nc6 11. Bd3 Qc7 12. Be2 O-O {The Greek gift doesn't work for White.} ( 13. O-O f6! 14. exf6 Rxf6 15. Bd2 Bd7 16. Nb3 Bd6 17. Nbd4 a6 18. Nxc6? Bxc6 19. Qc2 ( 19. Rfe1 fails to 19...e5 20. Nxe5 Re8 21. Bxh7+ Kxh7 22. Qh5+ Rh6 and Black is winning ) 19... Rf7! {Ignoring h7 as he is threatening to sacrifice on f3.} ) )
7... Qc7!? {7...Qa5+ was my original suggestion, but I have changed my mind as I now believe we should capture on c5 with the bishop.}
8. c3 {White can also play 8 Bd3 or 8 g4.}
    ( 8. Bd3 Bxc5! 9. Bxf5 exf5 {White can also play 10 Qxd5 or 10 O-O.} ( 10. Qxd5 {Just too risky.} 10... Be6 11. Qd3 O-O 12. Nc3 ( 12. O-O Bc4 {is a nice little trick that nets an exchange.} ) 12... Nc6 {winning back the material.} ) ( 10. O-O Be6 11. Nc3 h6 12. Bf4 Qd7 13. Qe2 Nc6 14. Rad1 O-O 15. Be3 Be7 {Black will continue with the straightforward plan of putting his rooks on the c- and d-files.} ) )
    ( 8. g4?! {This is crazy, but let's check it anyway.} 8... Be7 9. b4 ( 9. c4 Qg6 10. cxd5 Nxe5 11. Bf4 Nd3+ 12. Qxd3 Nxf4 13. Nbd2 exd5 14. Qxd5 Nc6 15. O-O-O Be6 16. Re1 Be7 {Black has magnificent compensation.} ) 9... Bd7! 10. Bb5 a5! {Undermining.} 11. c3 Nc6 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Bf4 g5 14. Bg3 h5 {And ...g5-g4 is in the air.} )
    ( 8. b4?! a5! 9. c3 axb4 10. cxb4 b6! {Would be an idyllic undermining.} )
8... Bxc5 9. Bd3 h6! {I am advocating such a radical approach because of the time White has lost in moving his dark-squared bishop around the board.}
10. Bc1 {White has undeveloped his bishop which can only be good news for Black.}
    ( 10. Bf4 g5! {After 10 Bf4 I want to play the radical 10...g5!.} )
10... Nc6 11. Bxf5 {One of three important options: 11 Bc1, 11 O-O, or 11 Bxf5.}
    ( 11. Bc1 ( 11. Bg3 Nc6 12. O-O Nxg3 13. hxg3 g4 14. Nh4 Nxe5 15. Bb5+ Bd7 16. Bxd7+ Qxd7 {Black will castle queenside and to safety.} ) 11... Nc6 12. O-O ( 12. Nxf5 exf5 13. Qxd5 Be7 14. O-O Be6 15. Qd3 Rd8 16. Qe2 g4 17. Ne1 Nxe5 18. Qc2 Bc4 {Black is incredibly active.} ) 12... g4! 13. Nfd2 ( 13. Bxf5? gxf3 {wins} ) 13... Nxe5 14. Qxg4 Ne3 15. fxe3 Qxe3+ 16. Kh1 Nxd3 17. Qg7? Rh7! {Excellent defence along the seventh rank.} 18. Qg8+ Bf8 {Black is completely safe.} )
    ( 11. O-O {Allows the greedy 11...Nxe5.} 11... Nxe5 12. Bxf5 Nxf3+ 13. Qxf3 exf5 14. Bf4 Qc6 15. Re1+ Be6 16. Nd2 O-O 17. Qg3 Kh7 {White has some compensation, but Black has a solid position.} )
11... exf5 12. O-O {If White grabs with 12 Qxd5 then after 12...b6 13 O-O O-O 14 Re1 Rd8 15 Qb3 Be6 16 Qc2 b5 17 a4...}
    ( 12. Qxd5 b6 13. O-O O-O 14. Re1 Rd8 15. Qb3 ( 15. Qc4 falls foul of the stinker 15...Nb4! ) 15... Be6 16. Qc2 b5 17. a4 ( 17. Nbd2 {and it's the same trick again: 17...Nb4.} ) 17... b4 18. Be3 Bxe3 19. Rxe3 Rac8 {He is struggling to develop.} 20. Nbd2 f4 21. Rd3 ( 21. Ree1 bxc3 22. Qxc3 Qb7 {The white queen is caught in the open.} ) 21... Nxe5 22. Nxe5 Qxe5 {Regains the pawn.} )
12... Be6! {For the time being the bishop can settle here, defending both f5 and d5. In the future we will looking to push ...d5-d4.}
13. Re1 {Blockading with 13 Nd4 can be met by 12...O-O 14 Be3 Nxe5 15 Bf4 g5 16 Bxe5 Qxe5 17 Re1 Qd6 18 Qd3 Qa6 19 Qxa6 bxa6 20 Nxe6 fxe8 21 Nd2 fxe6! with a wonderful endgame.}
    ( 13. b4 {Black can simply retreat the bishop.} 13... Be7 14. Re1 O-O 15. a4 Rac8 16. Na3? d4! {Undermining the b4-pawn.} )
13... O-O 14. Be3 {Alternatively, 14 Nbd2 Qb6 15 Re2 d4 16 Nb3 dxc3 17 Nxc5 Qxc5 and the position is favourable for Black.}
14... Bxe3 15. Rxe3 Rfd8 16. Na3 f4 17. Re1 Qb6 18. Qd2?! d4 {All our weaknesses have now been eliminated.}
19. cxd4 Nxd4 20. Qxf4 Nxf3+ 21. Qxf3 Qxb2 {Black is doing very well as the bishop rules over the white knight on a3.} *
